= Amaranth

_Status: a pre-Columbian American_ pseudocereal _and leaf-vegetable, foundational to_ Aztec _and_ Inca _diet, religion, and pharmacology — and the substance that, more than any other New World food, embodies the suppression-and-survival narrative of post-Conquest indigenous cuisine. Post-Columbian adoption into South Asia (as_ rāmdānā / chaulāī, the Hindu vrata fasting-day food) _and into the Caribbean and African diaspora (as_ callaloo) _is substantial. Status by tradition: classical in_ Mesoamerican (Aztec) _and_ Andean (Inca) _pre-Columbian medical-and-religious systems; post-classical in Ayurveda (treated by reasoned extension on the pseudocereal-and-vrata axis, alongside buckwheat — see buckwheat.typ); not classical in Chinese, Tibb, or Greco-Roman as a_ food _(though the Greco-Roman tradition treats the_ ornamental amaranth flower _briefly via Theophrastus and Pliny — a real but distinct substance from the New World grain amaranth)._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Amaranthus_ L., family Amaranthaceae. The medicinal-and-food species:
- *_A. caudatus_ L.* — *Andean kiwicha / love-lies-bleeding*; the foundational Inca grain.
- *_A. cruentus_ L.* — Mexican grain amaranth.
- *_A. hypochondriacus_ L.* — Aztec grain amaranth.
- *_A. tricolor_ L.* — *callaloo* / Indian _chaulāī_ greens; principal leaf-vegetable form.
- *_A. retroflexus_ L.* — pigweed; wild form.
- *_A. viridis_ L.* — wild Indian / Asian form.

The drug-foodstuffs are *the seed* (the pseudocereal use — botanically a single-seeded utricle, not a true grain; the species is in Amaranthaceae, *not* Poaceae) and *the young leaves* (the leaf-vegetable use; the leaves are eaten across the entire global amaranth-using tradition).

_Amaranthus_ from Greek ἀμάραντος (_amárantos_), "unfading" — α- (negative prefix) + μαραίνω (_maraínō_, "to wither, fade"). The Greek name refers to the *persistence of the flower-head*: amaranth flowers retain their bright red-purple color after drying, the basis of their classical ornamental and ritual use. The "unfading flower" was a classical Greek symbol of immortality; the *Amarantine* in Christian liturgy and the Pauline "crown of unfading glory" (1 Peter 5:4 — στέφανος ἀμαράντινος, _stéphanos amarántinos_) preserve the metaphor.#footnote[Etymology: Liddell & Scott, _A Greek–English Lexicon_ (s.v. _ἀμάραντος_, _μαραίνω_); Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _amarantus_); _Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "amaranth"). The "unfading flower" classical-Greek symbol is well documented; Pauline NT use at 1 Peter 5:4 and similar at 1 Peter 1:4 (κληρονομία ἀμάραντος, "inheritance unfading").]

*English:* _Amaranth_, from Latin _amarantus_; the older spelling _amarant_ persists in older botanical literature. The "h" was added in early modern English by analogy with Greek-Hellenic words. Common species names: _love-lies-bleeding_ (the pendulous purple-red _A. caudatus_ inflorescence — a memorable garden flower), _Joseph's coat_ (the multi-colored _A. tricolor_), _pigweed_ (the wild _A. retroflexus_).

*Nahuatl (Aztec):* *huautli* (also _huāuhtli_) — the Aztec name for the seed; foundational to Aztec religion and diet. Closely associated with the goddess *Cihuacōātl* and the war-god *Huitzilopochtli*; the Aztec ritual practice of forming *tzoalli* (a paste of amaranth flour mixed with human blood and/or maguey-honey, shaped into images of the gods to be eaten ritually) is the famous practice that led the Spanish authorities to *suppress amaranth cultivation* after the Conquest. The post-Conquest survival as *alegría* (Spanish for "joy/happiness") — popped amaranth seeds mixed with honey, sugar, or molasses into sweet bars — preserves the form (a sweet shaped block) while removing the blood, and is widely associated in modern Mexican-Catholic practice with *Día de los Muertos* (Day of the Dead). The substance carries a continuous syncretic-religious history from pre-Conquest Aztec ritual to modern Mexican folk Catholicism.#footnote[Huautli and Aztec religious practice: Sahagún, _Historia general de las cosas de Nueva España_ (16th c., the Florentine Codex); modern Aztec scholarship including Coe, _America's First Cuisines_ (1994); the suppression and the survival as alegría are extensively documented in Mexican food-history literature. The colonial suppression of amaranth cultivation is one of the principal cases of post-Conquest religious-dietary suppression.]

*Quechua (Inca / Andean):* *kiwicha* (also _quihuicha_, _qiwicha_) — the Andean name; the principal cultivated grain in the Andean diet alongside quinoa, kañiwa, and maize. Less suppressed than the Aztec huautli but with parallel post-Conquest decline; revived in the 20th c. as part of the broader Andean-grain renaissance.

*Sanskrit / Hindi:* *रामदाना (_rāmdānā_, "Rama's grain")* — the *post-Columbian* Indian name; the grain entered Indian use through Portuguese-colonial-period trade and was rapidly adopted as a *vrata fasting-day grain*, exactly paralleling the case of buckwheat (see buckwheat.typ). The naming "Rama's grain" reflects its religious-purity status. *चौलाई (_chaulāī_)* — the Hindi for the leafy amaranth used as a vegetable. *रामदाना / रामधन / रामबीज* (variants), *रामलड्डू (rāmladdū)* — the sweet ladoo made from popped rāmdānā with jaggery, a direct South-Asian parallel to Mexican alegría.

*Chinese:* 莧菜 (xiàncài) — generic amaranth (leaves); 籽粒莧 (zǐlì xiàn) — grain amaranth. The leaf form has *long Chinese use* (莧 in Bencao Gangmu) as a vegetable; the grain form is post-Columbian. Not in classical Chinese pharmacy as a major drug.

*Arabic / Persian:* قطف (_quṭuf_) — generic amaranth in some medieval Arabic botanical literature, but the identification is uncertain; the *grain amaranth* is post-classical to Tibb and has no canonical Avicennan entry. Modern Persian: تاج خروس (_tāj-e khorus_, "rooster's crown" — the inflorescence imagery) for the ornamental form.

*Caribbean / African diaspora:* *callaloo* — the term covers multiple leafy vegetables (also taro leaves and others); _A. tricolor_ and related species are the principal "callaloo" in Trinidad, Guyana, Jamaica, and broader Caribbean cuisine; the *African slave-trade transmission* of amaranth as leafy-vegetable food is well documented.

== Classical Mesoamerican (Aztec) and Andean (Inca) tradition

_The substantive pre-Columbian classical tradition for amaranth — not the secondary post-Columbian Old World adoption._

*Aztec medical-religious framework:*
- Huautli was *one of the four foundational grains* of the Aztec staple diet alongside maize (_centli / cintli_), beans (_etl_), and chia (_chian_).
- Religious centrality: huautli paste (_tzoalli_) was the foundational substance for forming sacred images during major festivals (especially _Panquetzaliztli_, the festival of Huitzilopochtli; *Tóxcatl*; and the *Bird-Hummingbird-of-the-Left* warrior cult). The ritually-prepared images were broken and consumed by the community.
- The Aztec ritual practice of *forming god-images from amaranth-and-blood paste* was understood by the Spanish colonial authorities as a parodic-blasphemous reflection of the Catholic Eucharist; the consequent *prohibition of amaranth cultivation* was strongly enforced through the 16th and early 17th centuries.
- Aztec medicinal use: huautli paste applied topically for skin disorders; the seeds as a strengthening food; the leaves as a cooked vegetable.
- *Survival as alegría*: in the 19th-20th c., Mexican folk-Catholic practice has reintegrated amaranth into the *Día de los Muertos* and other festival registers, with the alegría sweet (popped-amaranth-and-honey bars) carrying both the Aztec religious-ritual and the Catholic colonial-syncretic significance.#footnote[Aztec religion and food: Sahagún, _Florentine Codex_; modern Aztec-religion scholarship (e.g. Carrasco, _City of Sacrifice_). Colonial suppression: Spanish colonial-period documents and indigenous literature. The alegría / Day of the Dead survival is extensively documented in Mexican folk-religion and food-history literature.]

*Inca medical-religious framework:*
- Kiwicha was *one of the foundational Andean grains* alongside quinoa, kañiwa, maize, and the various tubers.
- Less suppressed than Aztec amaranth but with parallel post-Conquest decline; the substance was not as centrally implicated in Inca religious ritual and so escaped the most severe colonial restriction.
- Andean medicinal use: kiwicha porridge as a strengthening convalescent food; the seeds added to chicha (fermented corn beer) preparations; topical use of leaves for skin disorders.
- Modern revival: kiwicha is one of the principal grains of the 20th-c. Andean food-renaissance and global "ancient grains" movement.#footnote[Inca and Andean grains: standard archaeological and ethnobotanical literature (e.g. Murra, _Formaciones económicas y políticas del mundo andino_; Pearsall, _Plant Domestication in the Americas_). Kiwicha modern revival: 20th-c. agricultural-economic literature; the FAO has flagged kiwicha and the Andean grains as part of food-security strategy.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_The leaf form of amaranth (莧 xiàn, broadly_ A. tricolor _and related species) has Bencao tradition use as a vegetable; the grain form (籽粒莧 zǐlì xiàn) is post-Columbian and not in the canonical Bencao. The placement below covers the leaf form (canonical) and the grain (reasoned extension)._

*莧 / 莧菜 (xiàn / xiàncài, leaf form):*

*Property:* Cool (涼). *Flavor:* Sweet (甘), slightly bitter (微苦). *Channels:* Large Intestine (大腸), Small Intestine (小腸), Liver (肝) in some sources.

*Actions:* 清熱解毒 (clears heat and resolves toxicity), 通利二便 (promotes bowel and urination), 涼血止血 (cools blood and stops bleeding), 緩瀉 (mild laxative). Indicated for damp-heat dysentery, urinary disorders, eye irritation from Liver-heat, postpartum hemorrhage (the cooling-blood-stopping register).

*Cautions:* Spleen-vacuity-cold; chronic loose stools.

*Reasoned placement for the grain form*: by extension on the cereal-and-pseudocereal axis with attention to the unusual fat content (high squalene) and complete protein profile — sweet, neutral-to-cool, with mild astringent-binding character for the cooked porridge form; supports Spleen and Stomach without the cloying tendency of refined rice or wheat; appropriate for vacuity-recovery and the gluten-free constitution. Reasoned, not citation.#footnote[莧 / 莧菜 leaf form: Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_, 1578; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed., in modern vegetable-drug coverage. The grain form is post-Columbian to Chinese pharmacy and is not in the classical canon; the reasoned placement is interpretive.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_The leaf form (चौलाई_ chaulāī_, principally_ A. tricolor _and_ A. viridis_) has classical and folk Indian use as a vegetable; the_ Amaranthus _genus has Sanskrit names for several species including_ tāṇḍuliyaka _(_A. spinosus_) and_ marṣa _(generic). The_ grain form _(रामदाना_ rāmdānā_) is_ post-Columbian _and is treated by reasoned extension as a vrata-food parallel to buckwheat (see buckwheat.typ)._

*तण्डुलीयक (_tāṇḍulīyaka_)* — _A. spinosus_; classical Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu entry (_Śāka-varga_). Sanskrit synonyms also for the broader genus: मर्ष (_marṣa_), अल्पमारिष (_alpa-māriṣa_).

*Reasoned dravyaguna of the leaf form (chaulāī / tāṇḍulīyaka):*

*Rasa:* Madhura (sweet), Kaṣāya (astringent — secondary).

*Guṇa:* Laghu (light), Rūkṣa (dry).

*Vīrya:* Śīta (cooling).

*Vipāka:* Madhura (sweet).#footnote[Tāṇḍulīyaka dravyaguna: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Śāka-varga_; the cooling-light-dry leaf-vegetable profile is the standard classical placement.]

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies Pitta and Kapha; mildly Vāta-aggravating in excess (the laghu-rūkṣa).

*Karma:* _Rakta-pitta-hara_ (anti-bleeding from heat), _grāhī_ (binding mildly), _dāha-praśamana_ (relieves burning), _mūtrala_ (mild diuretic), _agni-dīpana_ (mild digestive), _viṣaghna_ (anti-toxin).

*Reasoned dravyaguna of the grain form (rāmdānā):*

By extension on the pseudocereal axis and parallel to buckwheat (see buckwheat.typ): madhura rasa with kaṣāya secondary; laghu-rūkṣa guṇa (lighter than refined rice or wheat); śīta vīrya (cooling); madhura vipāka. Pacifies Pitta and Kapha; aggravates Vāta if eaten without unctuous accompaniment.

*Prabhāva:* No classical assignment. _Reasoned commentarial:_ the *exceptionally high squalene content* (4-8% of seed oil — among the highest in any commonly-consumed plant food) gives the seed a *snigdha-bṛṃhaṇa* (oily-nourishing) register unusual for a pseudocereal, which classical commentary on a hypothetical canonical placement could plausibly read at prabhāva level; explicitly interpretive.

*Karma (reasoned):* _Laghu-pathya_ (light and wholesome for fasting); _bṛṃhaṇa_ (nourishing — the protein-and-squalene profile); _hṛd-rakṣaka_ (cardiotonic — modern, via the squalene fraction); _balya_ (strengthening); useful in pre- and post-vrata recovery; suitable for celiac and gluten-sensitive constitutions; pediatric and elderly convalescence.

*The vrata-food register*: rāmdānā joins buckwheat (_kuṭṭū_), water-chestnut flour (_siṅghāḍā_), and other non-true-cereal foods as permitted during *Hindu vrata fasting days* (especially Navarātri, Śivarātri, ekādaśī, Janmāṣṭamī). The technical religious-dietary classification rests on amaranth's *not being a true grain (anna)* in the Sanskrit dietetic sense; the same logic underlies the buckwheat case. Vrata cooking pairs rāmdānā with potato, sago (sabudana), and curd, with rock salt (sendhā namak) and the limited spice set permitted in the fast.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S, N, F, M)_

- *(N) Conditional cautions:* Vāta excess (the laghu-rūkṣa tendency); requires unctuous accompaniment (ghee, milk, coconut) in the fasting register.
- *(F) Folk:* heavy combinations with curd in some regional readings.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Oxalates:* amaranth leaves and seeds contain *moderate-to-high oxalate content* (especially the leaves); relevant for calcium-oxalate kidney-stone-formers and for severe oxalate-sensitivity. Cooking and discarding the cooking water reduces oxalate by ~30-50%.
  - *Nitrates:* leafy amaranth (especially _A. retroflexus_ wild pigweed) accumulates *high nitrate* concentrations; documented cases of *infant methemoglobinemia* from amaranth-leaf preparations given to young infants. Avoid heavy raw amaranth leaf intake in pre-weaning infants.
  - *Phytic acid:* moderate; reduced by soaking and proper cooking.
  - *Saponins:* trace; not clinically significant in normal use.
  - *Lectins:* present (as in many seeds); cooked grain is safe.
  - *Allergy:* documented but uncommon.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Amaranth is_ not classical in medieval Tibb _as a major food or drug. The_ Amaranthus _genus has limited mention in medieval Arabic botanical literature under names of uncertain identification (sometimes_ quṭuf _is glossed as a wild_ Amaranthus_, sometimes as_ Atriplex _or related Chenopodiaceae — the identification is contested); the New World grain amaranth is post-Columbian to Mediterranean and Levantine knowledge and post-classical to Tibb. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

Modern Arabic / Urdu naming for the grain: قطيفة (_quṭīfa_) and varieties; modern transliteration _amārant_ (أمارانت). South-Asian Unani has post-Columbian adoption of رامدانا (_rāmdānā_) parallel to the Ayurvedic case.

*Reasoned modern Unani placement* (entirely interpretive, post-classical):

*Mizāj* — by extension on the leafy-vegetable axis for the leaf form: *Cold and Moist in the 1st degree* (paralleling the Tibb classification of Mediterranean leafy vegetables like _silq_ chard and _khass_ lettuce); for the grain form, *Cold-Moist 1st degree* with *mughadhdhī* register from the protein-and-squalene content.

*Af'āl* (reasoned): the leaf: *mubarriḍ*, *muraṭṭib*, *mughadhdhī yasīr*, *mulayyin* (mild laxative); the grain: *mughadhdhī*, *muqawwī al-jasad*, *muraṭṭib*, *muḥsin al-dam* (blood-improving, the iron content), suitable for celiac and gluten-sensitive contexts.

*Istemālāt* (reasoned): convalescence; hot-pattern fever; gluten-free dietary necessity; vegetarian protein source.

*Mawāne'*: oxalate-sensitive conditions; infant pre-weaning (the nitrate concern).

The substance does not appear in Avicenna or Ibn al-Bayṭār; modern Unani usage is interpretive cross-system. See _tibb-al-arabi.typ_.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

The leaf form (莧菜) is principally a *dietetic vegetable* in Chinese tradition, not a compound-formula ingredient. The grain is not in classical formulas.

- *莧菜湯 (Xiàncài Tāng)* — amaranth-leaf soup; the classical Cantonese-style summer cooling vegetable preparation.
- *莧菜粥* — amaranth-leaf congee.
- Topical leaf preparations for skin disorders and snakebite (folk).

=== Ayurveda

The leaf form (chaulāī / tāṇḍulīyaka) is a classical _śāka_ (vegetable); the grain form (rāmdānā) has post-Columbian folk-religious-dietary use:

- *Chaulāī ki sabzī* — sautéed amaranth-leaf vegetable; the canonical North-Indian and Bihari preparation.
- *Tāṇḍulīyaka kvātha* — decoction; classical use for bleeding disorders and damp-heat dysentery.
- *Rāmdānā ladoo / Rāmladdū* — popped amaranth + jaggery (or honey) + ghee + cardamom; the canonical vrata sweet, paralleling Mexican alegría almost exactly.
- *Rāmdānā kheer* — popped amaranth + milk + sugar; vrata-day dessert.
- *Rāmdānā porridge* — boiled rāmdānā porridge with milk and jaggery; vrata-day breakfast.
- *Chaulāī-saag-rāmdānā mix* — leaves and grain combined as a complete-nutritional vrata dish in some regional traditions.

=== Mesoamerican (Aztec) / Andean (Inca) — the substantive classical traditions for amaranth as food-and-medicine

- *Alegría (Mexican)* — popped amaranth + honey or sugar or piloncillo + sometimes peanuts or pumpkin seeds, pressed into sweet bars; *the canonical Mexican folk preparation* and the direct survival of the Aztec _tzoalli_ form (with blood replaced by honey). Available year-round but especially associated with *Día de los Muertos* (1-2 November) and the broader Mexican-folk-Catholic festival calendar.
- *Atole de amaranto* — Mexican amaranth-flour drink (the broader _atole_ category of Mexican corn-and-grain drinks).
- *Tortillas de amaranto* — modern Mexican gluten-free tortillas.
- *Kiwicha porridge (Andean)* — boiled kiwicha with milk and sugar; the canonical Andean breakfast.
- *Chicha morada con kiwicha* — purple-corn drink with kiwicha addition.
- *Mazamorra de kiwicha* — Peruvian sweet pudding.
- *Pre-Columbian tzoalli ritual paste* — historical only; the practice was suppressed by the Spanish colonial authorities and is not continued in any modern context.

=== Western / Greco-Roman, European, and Caribbean / African traditions

- *Theophrastus and Pliny* mention _Amaranthus_ as an *ornamental flower* — the "unfading" garden plant; the food / grain use was unknown to the classical Mediterranean.
- *European garden ornamental tradition*: _A. caudatus_ (love-lies-bleeding) cultivated in European gardens from the 16th c. onward.
- *Caribbean callaloo*: _A. tricolor_ and related species as a leafy vegetable in Trinidad-and-Tobago (callaloo soup with okra and crab), Jamaica, Guyana. The substance arrived through African slave-trade transmission (the leafy-vegetable use is paralleled in West African food traditions for related Amaranthus species).
- *African leaf-vegetable tradition*: _A. cruentus_, _A. hybridus_, and related species are widely cultivated and consumed across sub-Saharan Africa as leafy vegetables, with the seed less often used.
- *Modern Western "ancient grain" adoption*: gluten-free, high-protein, complete amino-acid profile; rapid 21st-c. global market growth for amaranth flour and popped amaranth.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic) medicine

No classical Tibb formulas; modern Unani has minor post-Columbian adoption. See above.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Amaranth is principally a *hot-humid tropical / subtropical-humid* substance in its native American cultivation, with substantial *tropical-highland* (Andean kiwicha at 1,500-3,500m) variant. Post-Columbian cultivation in India is in *hot-humid* and *subtropical-humid* zones; in Africa, principally *hot-humid* and *Sahelian semi-arid*. See _climates.typ_.

*Hot-humid tropical (Mesoamerica — Mexico, Guatemala; Caribbean; South Asia; sub-Saharan Africa).* The native climate of the cultivated amaranth species; rapid summer growth, heat-tolerant, drought-tolerant relative to maize and wheat. In Ayurvedic _ṛtucaryā_ terms (Caraka Sūtra 6) the annual cycle is dominated by extended _grīṣma_ (summer-heat) and _varṣā_ (monsoon) loads on Pitta-Kapha; in 中醫 terms the climate is 暑濕 (summer-heat-damp) on the Heart-Spleen; in Tibb terms the constitution is Hot-Moist, loading on balgham and ṣafrā'. *The traditional response and the amaranth role*: the cooling-and-light leaf-vegetable (chaulāī, callaloo) is climatically apt; the grain provides a *high-protein, gluten-free, drought-tolerant, easily-popped staple* in seasons and regions where wheat and rice are unavailable. The Mesoamerican alegría sweet (popped seed + honey) is precisely calibrated to the climate — light, cooling-ish (despite the warming sugar), aromatic, providing fast energy for the warm-climate festival calendar.

*Tropical highland (Andean kiwicha — 1,500-3,500m elevation).* The Andean climate carries cool-to-cold nights and warm-to-hot days, with extreme diurnal range, low ambient humidity, and strong UV; the kiwicha plant is exceptionally well-adapted, producing the dense protein-and-squalene seed as a high-altitude survival mechanism. In Tibetan-Sowa-Rigpa-analogous terms (see _climates.typ_ for the framework), the highland climate carries Vāta-equivalent extreme dryness and cold loads; the *seed's snigdha-bṛṃhaṇa profile* (oily-nourishing, the squalene contribution) addresses this constitutionally. The Andean kiwicha-porridge breakfast is the climatically-appropriate winter-warming preparation; the alegría / kiwicha-honey-bar register provides high-density traveling food for the Andean-trail (Inca road system) and modern trekking contexts alike.

*Post-Columbian South Asian (hot-humid monsoonal).* The Indian adoption of rāmdānā into the *vrata fasting-day* register is climate-appropriate: the cooling-light grain is suitable for the late-summer monsoon-relief festival calendar (Navarātri occurs in the post-monsoon Aśvina month) and provides the gluten-free fasting-permitted carbohydrate that the religious-dietary classification requires. The South-Asian *rāmdānā ladoo* preparation parallels Mexican *alegría* almost exactly — popped grain + jaggery / honey + ghee + cardamom — without any documented cross-cultural transmission; the convergence is striking and reflects parallel saṃskāra reasoning on the same substance.

*Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26).* Amaranth exported globally as "ancient grain" and gluten-free flour reaches every climate. In cool-damp Atlantic European and cold-dry continental contexts, the *seed* form's protein-and-squalene density is climate-appropriate (cool-damp climates need bṛṃhaṇa-snigdha protein support); the *leaf* form's cooling character is less constitutionally aligned with cool-damp climates and is better suited to summer use even outside the tropics. The *celiac-disease and gluten-sensitivity* indication transcends climate — amaranth is climate-neutral for the gluten-free dietary necessity. *The vrata-food register* does not travel as such — it is religious-dietary and culturally specific — but the popped-amaranth-and-honey sweet (alegría / rāmladdū / kiwicha-bar) has become a *global health-food snack* with broad climate-independent acceptability.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Composition, amaranth grain, raw, approximate per 100 g* — verify against USDA FoodData Central.

- Energy ~370 kcal; Water ~10 g
- *Protein ~14 g* — *with a notably complete amino acid profile*; *lysine ~0.75 g (high for a pseudocereal — comparable to or higher than quinoa)*; methionine adequate.
- *Fat ~7 g* — *unusually high for a pseudocereal*; *squalene ~0.5-0.8 g (4-8% of the seed oil — among the highest plant sources)*; substantial oleic and linoleic acids.
- Carbohydrate ~65 g; Dietary fiber ~7 g.
- *Iron ~7 mg*; *Calcium ~160 mg* — high for a grain; Magnesium ~250 mg; Phosphorus ~560 mg.
- Folate ~80 µg; B vitamins broadly present.
- *Gluten-free* — celiac-safe, paralleling buckwheat.

*Leaves, raw, approximate per 100 g:*
- Energy ~25 kcal; Water ~90 g
- Protein ~2.5 g; Fat ~0.3 g; Carbohydrate ~4 g; Fiber ~2 g
- *Iron ~2.3 mg*; *Calcium ~215 mg* — very high; *Magnesium ~55 mg*; *Potassium ~610 mg*
- *Vitamin A and folate substantial*; vitamin C ~43 mg.
- *Oxalates moderate-to-high* — see Viruddha.
- *Nitrates moderate-to-high* in wild and some cultivated forms.#footnote[Composition: USDA FoodData Central; published amaranth-research literature in _Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry_, _Cereal Chemistry_, _Food Chemistry_. The squalene content is exceptional for plant foods — amaranth oil is one of the few plant sources approaching the squalene content of shark liver oil; the cardiovascular-research interest in plant squalene is substantial.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Squalene*: the principal modern pharmacological-research interest; precursor to cholesterol and vitamin D; cardiovascular-protective effects in preclinical studies; widely used as a vaccine adjuvant (MF59 in some influenza vaccines). The 4-8% seed-oil squalene of amaranth is *exceptional* among plant foods; the only comparable plant source is olive oil (~0.1-0.7%). Amaranth is the highest plant-squalene source.
- *Complete protein with lysine emphasis*: the canonical amaranth nutritional claim; lysine is the limiting amino acid in most cereals (wheat, maize, rice), so amaranth's high lysine makes it a clean cereal complement.
- *Gluten-free*: the celiac and gluten-sensitivity indication.
- *Iron and calcium*: significant dietary contribution.
- *Antioxidant*: phenolic compounds substantial; antioxidant activity well-characterized.
- *Anti-inflammatory*: preclinical evidence; modest clinical evidence for modest cholesterol-lowering with regular amaranth consumption.
- *Anti-hypertensive*: small clinical evidence; peptide-derived ACE-inhibitory activity from amaranth-protein digests.
- *Cancer-prevention*: substantial preclinical evidence for tocotrienol and squalene fractions; clinical translation preliminary.
- *Cross-system convergence*: the Aztec / Andean valorization as a strengthening food + the modern complete-protein-and-squalene profile + the Indian vrata adoption as a religious-pure light grain all converge on the *high-quality nutritive register*.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* No toxicological upper limit for the grain. Practical considerations:
- *Adult dietary use*: 30-50 g raw / 100-150 g cooked grain per serving; appropriate for daily / staple consumption.
- *Leaf use*: 100-200 g cooked leaf per serving; cook and discard cooking water for oxalate-sensitive individuals.
- *Risk groups*:
  - *Calcium-oxalate kidney-stone-formers*: limit heavy intake of leaves; the grain is lower in oxalate.
  - *Infants pre-weaning*: avoid heavy leafy-amaranth feeding (nitrate / methemoglobinemia concern).
  - *Amaranth-pollen allergy*: documented, especially for wild pigweed.
  - *Celiac patient*: *favors* amaranth (gluten-free).
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

